    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma\n",
    "\n",
    "::: {#thm-riemann-lebesgue name=\"Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma\"}\n",
    "Let $d\\in\\mathbb{N}$ and let $\\lambda_d$ denote Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb{R}^d$. If $f \\in L^1(\\mathbb{R}^d, \\mathcal{B}_{\\mathbb{R}^d}, \\lambda_d)$, then its Fourier transform\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{f}(\\xi) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f(x) e^{i \\langle x, \\xi \\rangle} d\\lambda_d(x).\n",
    "$$\n",
    "belongs to $C_0(\\mathbb{R}^d)$. That is, $\\hat{f}$ is continuous and \n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\lim_{\\|\\xi\\| \\to \\infty} |\\hat{f}(\\xi)| = 0.\n",
    "$$\n",
    ":::\n",
    "\n",
    "**Some Observations:**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. The central message of the Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma is that the Fourier transform of an integrable function vanishes at infinity.\n",
    "2. More precisely, if $f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^d)$, then $\\hat f(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}f(x)e^{i\\langle x,\\xi\\rangle}\\,dx$ satisfies\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "\\boxed{\\|\\xi\\|\\to\\infty \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\hat f(\\xi)\\to0.} \n",
    "$$\n",
    "3. The factor $e^{i\\langle x,\\xi\\rangle}$ oscillates increasingly rapidly as $\\|\\xi\\|$ becomes large. When integrated against an integrable function $f(x)$, the positive and negative/complex oscillations increasingly cancel each other:\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "\\begin{aligned}\n",
    "&\\text{high frequency} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\text{rapid oscillation} \\\\\n",
    "&\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\text{cancellation} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\hat f(\\xi)\\approx0. \n",
    "\\end{aligned}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "4. This has a particularly important interpretation for characteristic functions.\n",
    "If $X$ has an $L^1$ density $f$, then $\\varphi_X(\\xi)=\\hat f(\\xi)$ so\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "\\boxed{\\varphi_X(\\xi)\\to0 \\qquad\\text{as }\\|\\xi\\|\\to\\infty.} \n",
    "$$\n",
    "However, the converse is not true: a characteristic function can tend to zero even when the distribution does not have a density. Thus the Riemann–Lebesgue lemma gives a sufficient condition, not a characterization.\n",
    "\n",
    "5. The lemma expresses a fundamental principle of Fourier analysis:\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "\\boxed{ L^1(\\mathbb R^d) \\overset{\\mathcal F}{\\longrightarrow} C_0(\\mathbb R^d). } \n",
    "$$\n",
    "That is, Fourier transformation converts integrable functions into continuous functions that vanish at infinity.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Proof of the Riemann-Lebesgue Lemma\n",
    "\n",
    "The proof proceeds in three main steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 1: Smooth Compactly Supported Functions ($f \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$)\n",
    "\n",
    "Assume first that $f \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$. Let $\\xi\\ne0$ and choose $j$ such that $|\\xi_j|=\\|\\xi\\|_\\infty$, so that $\\xi_j\\ne0$. By Fubini's theorem,\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "\\hat f(\\xi) = \\int_{\\mathbb R^{d-1}} \\left[ \\int_{\\mathbb R} f(x_j,x_{-j}) e^{i x_j\\xi_j} \\,dx_j \\right] e^{i\\sum_{k\\neq j}x_k\\xi_k} \\,dx_{-j}. \n",
    "$$\n",
    "Now focus on the inner integral $I(x_{-j}) = \\int_{\\mathbb R} f(x_j,x_{-j})e^{i x_j\\xi_j}\\,dx_j$. Since $\\xi_j\\neq0$,\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "e^{i x_j\\xi_j} = \\frac{1}{i\\xi_j} \\frac{d}{dx_j}e^{i x_j\\xi_j}. \n",
    "$$\n",
    "Therefore\n",
    "$$\n",
    "I(x_{-j}) = \\frac{1}{i\\xi_j} \\int_{\\mathbb R} f(x_j,x_{-j}) \\frac{d}{dx_j}e^{i x_j\\xi_j}\\,dx_j. \n",
    "$$\n",
    "Apply the one-dimensional integration-by-parts formula $\\int u\\,dv=uv-\\int v\\,du$ with\n",
    "$u=f(x_j,x_{-j})$ and $dv=\\frac{d}{dx_j}e^{i x_j\\xi_j}\\,dx_j$. Then\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "du= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j}(x_j,x_{-j})\\,dx_j, \\qquad v=e^{i x_j\\xi_j}. \n",
    "$$\n",
    "Thus\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "\\begin{aligned} \n",
    "I(x_{-j}) &= \\frac{1}{i\\xi_j} \\left[ f(x_j,x_{-j})e^{i x_j\\xi_j} \\right]_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\\\ \n",
    "&\\qquad -\\frac{1}{i\\xi_j} \\int_{\\mathbb R} \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j}(x_j,x_{-j}) e^{i x_j\\xi_j}\\,dx_j. \n",
    "\\end{aligned} \n",
    "$$\n",
    "Here the boundary term vanishes because \\(f\\) has compact support; that is, \\(f(x_j,x_{-j}) = 0\\) for \\(|x_j|\\) sufficiently large. Consequently, $\\left[ f(x_j,x_{-j})e^{i x_j\\xi_j} \\right]_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} =0$. Therefore,\n",
    "$$ \n",
    "I(x_{-j}) = -\\frac{1}{i\\xi_j} \\int_{\\mathbb R} \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j}(x_j,x_{-j}) e^{i x_j\\xi_j}\\,dx_j. \n",
    "$$\n",
    "Substitute $I(x_{-j})$ back into the $d$-dimensional integral yields\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\hat{f}(\\xi) = \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} f(x) e^{i \\langle x, \\xi \\rangle} d\\lambda(x) = \\frac{1}{-i \\xi_j} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j}(x) e^{i \\langle x, \\xi \\rangle} d\\lambda(x)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Taking absolute values yields:\n",
    "$$\n",
    "|\\hat{f}(\\xi)| \\le \\frac{1}{|\\xi_j|} \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} \\left| \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j}(x) \\right| d\\lambda(x) = \\frac{1}{|\\xi_j|} \\left\\| \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j} \\right\\|_{L^1}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Since $\\|\\xi\\|_\\infty = \\max_{1 \\le j \\le d} |\\xi_j| \\ge \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d}} \\|\\xi\\|_2$, we choose index $j$ such that $|\\xi_j| = \\|\\xi\\|_\\infty$. Consequently:\n",
    "$$|\\hat{f}(\\xi)| \\le \\frac{\\sqrt{d}}{\\|\\xi\\|_2} \\max_{1 \\le j \\le d} \\left\\| \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_j} \\right\\|_{L^1} \\xrightarrow{\\|\\xi\\| \\to \\infty} 0$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus, $\\hat{f}(\\xi) = O(\\|\\xi\\|^{-1}) \\to 0$ as $\\|\\xi\\| \\to \\infty$ for all $f \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 2: Density of $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ in $L^1(\\mathbb{R}^d)$\n",
    "\n",
    "We must extend the result to arbitrary functions $f \\in L^1(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ by showing $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ is dense in $L^1(\\mathbb{R}^d)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Magnitude Truncation:** By the Dominated Convergence Theorem (DCT), $f \\cdot \\mathbf{1}_{\\{|f| \\le M\\}} \\to f$ in $L^1$ as $M \\to \\infty$. Thus, we may assume $f$ is bounded.\n",
    "2. **Support Truncation:** Again by DCT, $f \\cdot \\mathbf{1}_{\\overline{B}_R} \\to f$ in $L^1$ as $R \\to \\infty$. Thus, we may assume $f$ has compact support on $\\overline{B}_R$.\n",
    "3. **Approximation on a finite-measure set:** Put $K=\\overline{B}_R$. Every bounded Borel function on $K$ can be approximated in $L^1(K,\\lambda_d)$ by smooth compactly supported functions on $\\mathbb{R}^d$. Indeed, by approximation by bounded simple functions, it is enough to approximate indicators of Borel sets $A\\subset K$. By regularity of Lebesgue measure, for every $\\delta>0$ there are a compact set $F\\subset A$ and an open set $G\\supset A$ such that $\\lambda_d(G\\setminus F)<\\delta$. A smooth cutoff function $\\eta\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ can be chosen with $0\\le\\eta\\le1$, $\\eta=1$ on $F$, and $\\operatorname{supp}(\\eta)\\subset G$. Consequently,\n",
    "   $$\\|\\mathbf{1}_A-\\eta\\|_{L^1(K)}\\le\\lambda_d(G\\setminus F)<\\delta.$$\n",
    "   Applying this to the finitely many level sets of a simple-function approximation gives, for every $\\varepsilon>0$, a $\\psi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ such that\n",
    "$$\\|f - \\psi\\|_{L^1} < \\varepsilon$$\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Step 3: Synthesis ($\\varepsilon / 2$ Argument)\n",
    "\n",
    "Given $f \\in L^1(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Choose $\\psi \\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ such that $\\|f - \\psi\\|_{L^1} < \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}$.\n",
    "2. For any $\\xi \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$, estimate:\n",
    "   $$|\\hat{f}(\\xi)| \\le |\\hat{f}(\\xi) - \\hat{\\psi}(\\xi)| + |\\hat{\\psi}(\\xi)| = |\\widehat{f - \\psi}(\\xi)| + |\\hat{\\psi}(\\xi)|$$\n",
    "3. Note that $|\\widehat{f - \\psi}(\\xi)| \\le \\|f - \\psi\\|_{L^1} < \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}$.\n",
    "4. By Step 1, $\\hat{\\psi} \\in C_0(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, so there exists $R > 0$ such that for all $\\|\\xi\\| \\ge R$, $|\\hat{\\psi}(\\xi)| < \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Combining these inequalities:\n",
    "$$|\\hat{f}(\\xi)| < \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2} + \\frac{\\varepsilon}{2} = \\varepsilon \\quad \\text{for all } \\|\\xi\\| \\ge R$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Hence, $\\lim_{\\|\\xi\\| \\to \\infty} \\hat{f}(\\xi) = 0$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Continuity of $\\hat{f}$:** For any $\\xi_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$, we have $|\\hat{f}(\\xi) - \\hat{f}(\\xi_0)| \\le \\int_{\\mathbb{R}^d} |f(x)| \\, |e^{i\\langle x, \\xi\\rangle} - e^{i\\langle x, \\xi_0\\rangle}| \\, d\\lambda(x)$. As $\\xi \\to \\xi_0$, the integrand converges pointwise to $0$ and is dominated by $2|f(x)| \\in L^1$, so DCT gives $\\hat{f}(\\xi) \\to \\hat{f}(\\xi_0)$. Thus $\\hat{f} \\in C_0(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, completing the proof."
   ]
